          ],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.099,
                0.494,
                0.723,
                0.591
              ],
              "content": "and it requires one more electron to complete its octet. If sodium and chlorine were to react, the electron lost by sodium could be taken up by chlorine. After gaining an electron, the chlorine atom gets a unit negative charge, because its nucleus has 17 protons and there are 18 electrons in its K, L and M shells. This gives us a chloride anion C1–. So both these elements can have a give-and-take relation between them as follows (Fig. 3.5)."
            }
          ],
